Endoscopy Department | 123 Health St, Suite 400, Wellness City, ZZ 12345 | Phone: (555) 123-4567 | Fax: (555) 765-4321

Procedure: The patient was positioned in the left lateral decubitus position. A bite block was placed. Using a Olympus CF-H180AL endoscope, we carefully traversed the oropharynx and passed the upper esophageal sphincter.

Esophagus: The esophageal mucosa appeared normal. The Z-line was identified at 38 cm from the incisors. The gastroesophageal junction (GEJ) appeared normal without any evidence of rings, strictures, or esophagitis.

Stomach: Upon entering the stomach, the fundus, body, and antrum were examined. Notable for mild erythema in the antrum, which was consistent with mild gastritis. Normal rugal folds with adequate insufflation. The pylorus was easily traversed.

Duodenum: The first and second parts of the duodenum were examined. The villi appeared normal. No ulcers or significant inflammation were observed.

